@section boards_status Validation status

| Status | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| <span class="hc-badge hc-ok">QEMU tested in CI</span> | CI builds Debug and Release, then runs `tests/qemu_files_test.py` against the emulated board: system and file commands, sessions, create/remove cycles, and BASIC at the nesting limit. |
| <span class="hc-badge hc-ok">Builds in CI</span> | CI cross-compiles Debug and Release. Nothing executes the firmware. |
| <span class="hc-badge hc-pending">Hardware pending</span> | The port targets a physical board but has not been run on one. A successful build does not show that clocks, pins, or the UART work. |
| <span class="hc-badge hc-info">Emulator only</span> | The port relies on QEMU's reset state and does not configure real hardware. |

@section boards_matrix Feature support

Every board runs the same firmware features. The differences are in clocking,
console wiring, memory limits, and how each board is validated.

| Feature | LM3S6965EVB | STM32F4DISCOVERY | STM32VLDISCOVERY |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Shell, sessions, RAM files, G2BASIC | Yes | Yes | Yes (BASIC nesting 4, files up to 1 KB) |
| 1 kHz SysTick uptime | Yes, guest time | Yes, HSI accuracy | Yes |
| Console | UART0 (IRQ receive) | USART2 (IRQ receive) | USART1 (IRQ receive) |
| VFS device nodes | `/dev/uart0` to `/dev/uart2` | `/dev/uart0` | `/dev/uart0` |
| Clock setup | None (QEMU reset state) | HSI, 16 MHz | PLL to 24 MHz (skipped in the QEMU build) |
| Main stack | 3 KB | 3 KB | 2 KB |
| Buffered stdio | Yes | Yes | No (saves about 2 KB of heap) |
| QEMU machine | `lm3s6965evb` | None | `stm32vldiscovery` |
| CI | Build + QEMU regression | Build | Build both presets + QEMU regression |
| Physical hardware | Not covered | Pending | Pending |

No board has a scheduler, privilege separation, authentication, persistent
storage, or interrupt-driven UART I/O. The SVC and PendSV handlers panic on all
targets.

@section boards_memory Memory

@htmlinclude[block] memory-overview.svg

The 8 KB STM32VLDISCOVERY sets the lower bound for shell and VFS memory use.
Its defconfig reduces the stack, file size, and BASIC nesting limits; see
@ref board_stm32vldiscovery_memory "its memory budget".

@section boards_presets Build presets

Configure and build a preset from the repository root with the project venv, as
described in the [development guide](https://github.com/grzegorz-grzeda/home-core/blob/main/docs/development.md):

```bash
cmake --preset <preset> -DPython3_EXECUTABLE="$PWD/.venv/bin/python"
cmake --build --preset <preset>
```

| Preset | Board | Defconfig |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `lm3s6965evb` | LM3S6965EVB | `configs/homecore_defconfig` |
| `lm3s6965evb-release` | LM3S6965EVB, Release | `configs/homecore_defconfig` |
| `stm32f4discovery` | STM32F4DISCOVERY | `configs/homecore_defconfig` |
| `stm32vldiscovery` | STM32VLDISCOVERY | `configs/stm32vldiscovery_defconfig` |
| `stm32vldiscovery-qemu` | STM32VLDISCOVERY in QEMU | `configs/stm32vldiscovery_defconfig` + overlay `src/board/stm32vldiscovery/qemu.yaml` |

Each preset builds into `build/<preset>/`, writing the ELF `homecore` and the
raw image `homecore.bin`. A board
uses `configs/<board>_defconfig` when that file exists and
`configs/homecore_defconfig` otherwise.

@section boards_adding Adding a board

New boards, SoCs, and CPUs follow the
[porting guide](https://github.com/grzegorz-grzeda/home-core/blob/main/docs/porting.md).
Add a page for the new board here, alongside its Markdown guide in `docs/boards/`.

@subpage board_lm3s6965evb <br>
@subpage board_stm32f4discovery <br>
@subpage board_stm32vldiscovery
*/
